{
  "id": "phase-100",
  "title": "Add path handling so programs stop concatenating separators by hand",
  "severity": "major",
  "depends_on": [
    "phase-090"
  ],
  "risk": "medium",
  "finding": "Every program that touches the filesystem builds paths by string concatenation, and Redblue offers nothing to help. src/stdlib.rs exposes files.read, files.write, files.append, files.exists, files.lines, files.delete, files.copy and files.rename - all of which take a string path, and none of which understands a directory. There is no join, no parent, no filename, no extension, no directory listing and no notion of a working directory. So `a + \"/\" + b` appears in every script, breaks on Windows where the separator is not `/`, and there is no way to walk a tree. Phase-050 already had to fix a Windows backslash path bug of exactly this shape in tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs.",
  "goal": "Add a path value with join, parent, filename, stem, extension, is_absolute, resolve and a directory listing, and route the files module's existing functions through it so they accept a path value as well as a string without breaking a single existing call. Separators must come from the platform rather than being hardcoded, and the tests must prove it rather than assume it - a test that only runs on Linux cannot catch a Windows separator bug. Keep the string form working, because the corpus and every existing program use it.",
  "accept": [
    "join, parent, filename, stem, extension and is_absolute each return the correct value for relative and absolute inputs, asserted literally",
    "the separator is taken from the platform and a test asserts the Windows and POSIX results differ in exactly the way the platform does - a test that cannot distinguish them fails",
    "a path value is accepted by files.read, files.write and files.exists, and every existing corpus program and test still passes unmodified",
    "a directory listing returns entries sorted, so the result is deterministic, and an empty directory returns an empty list rather than an error",
    "joining an absolute path onto a relative one has a documented rule that SPEC.md states and a test pins",
    "parent of a root path and filename of a trailing-slash path are documented edge cases with their answers asserted",
    "the tree-walker and the bytecode VM agree, compared as data across both engines",
    "edge_* tests cover a trailing separator, a double separator, a dot-segment, a path with spaces and a non-ASCII filename"
  ],
  "timeout_min": 180,
  "must_touch": [
    "src/"
  ]
}
